    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Henry Bolte** (65.40) and **Seiya Suzuki** (66.34) anchoring our outfield strategy. **Henry Bolte** is our prime play; facing Bennett in a favorable park with wind blowing out, his combination of an elite batting spot (#1) and a platoon advantage provides the highest floor on the roster. Similarly, **Seiya Suzuki** thrives in his matchup against Seth Lugo. Despite a moderate hold-rate from the pitcher, the 4.66 xERA and a favorable batting order position make him a mandatory start to secure our standing in Roto categories. **Corbin Carroll** (65.79) remains a high-upside play against Yamamoto; while the SP presents a tough challenge, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s volume and position in the two-hole keep his efficiency high, even with the projected drag from Yamamoto\u2019s ability to suppress steals.\n\nOur biggest bench dilemma was **Travis Bazzana** (47.82), who fell just shy of the cutoff. While he benefits from the #1 spot against a vulnerable arm in Kay, his platoon disadvantage (L/L) and lower base projection compared to our starters ultimately pushed him to the pine. We are also closely monitoring the lineup for **Isaac Paredes** (67.96) and **Brice Turang** (62.68); should their anticipated lineup spots drop, their efficiency scores will shift, though both are currently positioned for success against King and Taj Bradley, respectively. **Jose Caballero** and **Austin Riley** are starts to watch carefully, as their low lineup positioning (#8) severely caps their run-scoring potential, despite otherwise decent matchup data.\n\nInvestors should remain wary of the heavy reliance on \"Lineup Pending\" status for key contributors like **Isaac Paredes**, **Brice Turang**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Josh Naylor**. Any late-scratch announcements will necessitate immediate re-optimization to avoid dead slots. Furthermore, while **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** carry solid scores, both must contend with Yamamoto\u2019s elite profile (3.59 xERA), which inherently carries a higher strikeout risk that could suppress our batting average. Outside of the active roster, **James Wood** remains sidelined on the IL, leaving the bench depth limited for emergency pivots.",
